<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  A quire is a gathering of folded sheets — the unit a book is actually built from,
  nested one inside another and stitched at the fold. The mark is that fold: one
  sheet, two leaves. It reads as an open book at launcher size and is not the
  stack-with-a-spine every other reading app draws.

  (The app was called Folio first, which is the name for a single such sheet. The
  drawing did not have to change when the name did — a quire is made of folios.)

  Three details do the work, and all three are the difference between paper and a
  symbol of paper:

  - The fold is narrowest at mid-height and opens out towards the head and tail. A
    parallel slot reads as two rectangles set side by side; a taper reads as one
    sheet creased down the middle.
  - The head and tail edges wave rather than run straight, because a leaf lying open
    is never flat.
  - The left leaf is the same page colour held back a little. That is the gutter
    shadow — one sheet with light falling across it, not two pieces of card. Alpha
    rather than a second colour, so the tonal split survives when Android 13+ tints
    the whole mark one colour for a themed icon.

  108dp viewport. Everything sits inside the 66dp safe circle — centre (54,54),
  radius 33 — because every launcher masks an adaptive icon differently, and a mark
  that strays outside has its corner shaved on somebody's phone and nowhere else.
  LauncherMarkTest holds that line, bounding the curves by their control points.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
